HOW TO RUN THIS DISK====================This disk is NOT a boot disk, and os9exec does not boot anything.  There isno kernel here and no OS9Boot file.  os9exec is the kernel: you point it at adisk with OS9DISK and name a program to run, and that disk is what OS-9 thensees as /dd -- its root and its home.  There is no established name for thatrole, which is why people reach for "boot disk"; this disk cannot bootanything.Of the 597 programs in CMDS and CMDS/GAMES, 495 need nothing but this disk.------------------------------------------------------Trying things before you fetch cio------------------------------------------------------If you run OS-9 you have cio, csl and math -- but the SDK may be on a shelfrather than to hand.  This is what works in the meantime, so you can lookaround the disk straight away:    a shell        sh, or bash    list a dir     ls    read a file    cat, or less    copy a file    cat one > twocp, mv, rm, mkdir and dir are starred -- they are the GNU ones, and theywant cio like the other starred programs.  Under os9exec the emulator hasits own copy, move, rename and makdir, which need nothing loaded at all:    os9exec -r icopy   <from> <to>    os9exec -r imakdir <dir>    os9exec -r move    <from> <to>    os9exec -r rename  <old> <new>Those are commands of the EMULATOR, not programs on the disk, so they aretyped where you start os9exec rather than at a shell prompt inside it.On your own hardware the equivalents are the ones you already have: copy,makdir, del, list and dir.cio, csl and math now SHIP on this disk, with Microware's permission, sothe starred programs run too and nothing here is special.  DOC/README-CIOhas the detail.293 of the 597 are starred in DOC/INDEX, meaning they use Microware's cio(see DOC/README-CIO).  They run: the module is here.WHERE THE PROGRAMS THINK THEY LIVE----------------------------------111 of them carry a hardcoded /h0 path.  That is not damage: they were builtby people whose freeware lived on /h0.84 of the 111 want just one file, /h0/sys/termcap -- and every one of thosereads the TERMCAP environment variable first.  SYS/login sets it to thisdisk's own copy, so those 69 work wherever the disk is mounted, with no /h0in sight.  That was measured, program by program, with no exceptions.What is left is 39 programs wanting data of their own under /h0, like/h0/games/hack/playground; most of the 39 are the gcc passes and thelinker.  Going the other way, 174 programs read their data from /dd --advent wants /dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz, fortune /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat,nroff /dd/LIB/tmac.*.No single arrangement satisfies both sets, which is what the three below arereally about.  DOC/DEPENDS lists every path each program opens.IF YOU WANT THE SHORT ANSWER: mount this collection as /dd, and hard-linkit as /h0 as well.  174 programs want their data at /dd against 39 thatwant it at /h0, so /dd is where the collection belongs, and the extra linkcosts one inode and collects the other 39.  That is arrangement 2.------------------------------------------------------------------1. YOU ALREADY RUN OS-9, AND WANT TO KEEP YOUR OWN /dd------------------------------------------------------------------Keep your own system as /dd and mount this collection as /h0:    OS9DISK=<your own disk>  OS9H0=<this image, named h0>  os9exec shellThen add this disk's commands to your path, in your own startup:    setenv PATH /dd/CMDS:/h0/CMDS:/h0/CMDS/GAMESPATH works in the OS-9 shell -- a bare command name is found along it, notonly in the execution directory.  (Verified: with /h0/CMDS on PATH a bare`rndname' runs; with it removed the same command gives E_PNNF.)What it buys you:  - /dd stays yours -- your CMDS, your LIB, your startup, your cio.  - Your own cio is used rather than the copy here, if you prefer it.  - Every hardcoded /h0 path resolves against this disk, where its data is.What it costs, and it is NOT a small cost: 174 of the 597 programs readdata from /dd -- advent its cave, fortune its database, nroff its macrofiles -- and under this arrangement /dd is YOURS, so they will look onyour disk and not find them.  This is the single biggest reason toprefer arrangement 2.  If you want a few of them here anyway, copy whatDOC/DEPENDS lists for each onto your own /dd, keeping the same/dd-relative places.For a shell with this disk's commands, TERMCAP and a working cd already setup, hand bash the login script -- it works out where the disk is mountedfrom the path you give it:    os9exec -r bash /h0/SYS/loginThe image must be a file NAMED h0: os9exec resolves an image by filename, soOS9H0 pointing at a file called anything else fails with error 214.Two things to know:  - If /h0 is already YOUR disk, keep it that way and see arrangement 3.  - The image must stay writable.  hack, larn and cribbage write into    /h0/games/*/playground and criblog.------------------------------------------------------------------2. THIS DISK AS /dd  --  recommended------------------------------------------------------------------Run this disk by itself.  It carries public-domain shells, so no Microwaremodule is needed anywhere:    OS9DISK=<this image>  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginThat trailing /dd/SYS/login matters.  Started bare, bash has no PATH, noHOME and no TERM, so it finds no command; SYS/login exports those and handsover to an interactive shell.  Setting HOME is also what makes bash read/dd/.bashrc, where cd, pwd and the aliases are defined.This gets you very nearly everything: all 597 programs in CMDS andCMDS/GAMES, starred or not, because the five Microware modules ship here;the 174 that read their data from /dd, because /dd is this disk; and --because SYS/login sets TERMCAP -- the 84 that would otherwise want/h0/sys/termcap.  Only the 39 with other /h0 data are out of reach, andthey are mostly compiler passes.If you want those 39 as well, give os9exec the image under a second name:    ln -s osk-freeware.dd h0    OS9DISK=<...>/osk-freeware.dd  OS9H0=<...>/h0  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginos9exec will not mount one path as two devices, so it needs two names; asymlink is enough and costs nothing.  Understand what it is, though: ONEimage behind two device names, each with its own sector cache.  Reading isfine.  Writing through both at once is not, so do not count on a scorewritten via /h0 being visible via /dd in the same session.Caveats when running the disk alone:  - PATH search works from the IMAGE and not from a host directory.  Run    the .dd and a bare `cookie' is found; point OS9DISK at an unpacked    directory instead and the same command says "command not found" with    PATH set and the program sitting there.  Reading .bashrc fails the same    way, with E$Unit.  Full paths always work.  - `sh', which the startup script names as its shell, starts but cannot    fork ANY external command.  It sees the file, agrees it is executable,    and the fork still fails.  Use bash.  - `ksh' is the nicest of the three but wants cio loaded before it starts,    which needs a shell first -- so it cannot be the first shell.------------------------------------------------------------------3. TAKE ONLY WHAT YOU WANT------------------------------------------------------------------Nobody needs SNOBOL, FORTRAN and XLISP at once.  To move a few programs ontoyour own disk and keep your own /h0:  - Copy this disk's GAMES/ to your own disk, so /h0/games/... resolves    against yours.  That is what the game data paths expect.  - Read DOC/DEPENDS before copying any single program.  It lists, per    program, every absolute path that program carries -- generated by    scanning the binaries.  110 of them need something besides their own    binary: a dictionary, a skeleton file, a scratch directory.  Copying    just the command often is not enough.  - Your own /h0/sys/termcap is then the one that gets used, which is    probably better tuned for your terminal than ours.  Or set TERMCAP and    the question does not arise.------------------------------------------------------------------THE SHELL YOU GET------------------------------------------------------------------bash 1.12 with /dd/.bashrc read, which matters more than it sounds.ALWAYS HAND BASH THE LOGIN SCRIPT.  Started bare --`os9exec /h0/CMDS/bash' -- you get a shell with no PATH, so `ls' is notfound; no HOME, so .bashrc is never read and bash complains about/.bashrc; and a `cd' that ABORTS THE SHELL on first use.  Everythingbelow depends on SYS/login having run.With no OS9DISK set, /dd is a host directory rather than an RBF disk, andbash prints `shell-init: getwd: cannot access parent directories' once atstartup.  It is harmless and there is nothing to fix: SYS/login spends thesame failure deliberately, in a script where it cannot be fatal, which isexactly what leaves cd working afterwards.What you get:  - cd and pwd WORK, but only because .bashrc replaces them.  bash's own    getwd() walks `..' upward looking for a directory that is its own    parent.  OS-9 has one root per DEVICE and nothing above them, so that    walk never ends: plain `pwd' HANGS THE SHELL, and where /dd is not an    RBF device plain `cd' aborts it with a bus error.  If a terminal here    has ever had to be closed rather than exited, this is the likeliest    reason.  The replacements track the path by string instead.  - ls, cat, less, wc, basename and dirname all run with no cio.  dir,    edir and l are the Microware-style listers and are starred; ls is the    one to use.  - vi_nocio is the editor that needs nothing -- PVic, cio-free, and it    drives a vt100 without any termcap file at all.  Plain `vi' is the EFFO    build and vi_cio is starred.  - `exit' leaves.  So does `quit' in most of the games; read DOC/INDEX    before starting one you cannot get out of.DOC/INDEX says what each program is; DOC/ORIGINS says where it came from;SOURCES.txt records the terms it arrived under.